  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=30$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,30)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,30).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,30)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,30)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,30):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡30+19≡19 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡19\\cdot 7≡133≡4\\cdot 30+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30) \\\\ {7}^{4} & ≡13\\cdot 7≡91≡3\\cdot 30+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $13\\cdot 7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,30),$ which means that $13$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $30$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $13,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30) \\\\ 13\\cdot 7x & ≡13\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30) \\\\ (13\\cdot 7)x & ≡78 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 30+18 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30) \\\\ x & ≡18 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,30)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,30).$",
